---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "@/components/Breadcrumb.astro";
import Main from "@/components/Main.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";

const pageTitle = "Privacy and Cookies";
const pageDescription =
  "How Richard W. Bown handles your data on humansoftwarebook.com: analytics cookies, the newsletter, hosting logs and your rights.";
---

<Layout
  title={`${pageTitle} | Human Software`}
  description={pageDescription}
>
  <Header />
  <Breadcrumb />
  <Main
    pageTitle={pageTitle}
    pageDesc="What I collect, why, and how to say no."
    class="app-prose humansoftware-privacy"
  >
    <p><em>Last updated 19 November 2026.</em></p>

    <h2 id="who">Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      This site is run by Richard W. Bown, who is the data controller for it.
      You can reach me at
      <a href="mailto:me@richardbown.com">me@richardbown.com</a> about anything
      on this page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="analytics">Analytics cookies</h2>
    <p>
      I use Google Analytics 4 (Google Ireland Limited, with Google LLC in the
      United States) to understand which pages people read and which links they
      follow. It is the only tracking on this site.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Nothing is loaded until you agree.</strong> On your first visit,
      the analytics script is not requested at all. If you accept, Google
      Analytics sets the following cookies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>_ga</code> — tells one browser apart from another. Expires after
        two years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>_ga_T9GQ4H4N86</code> — keeps a session’s state. Expires after two
        years.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      I do not use advertising or remarketing cookies, I do not build profiles
      across other websites, and I do not sell or share data with anyone.
      Analytics is configured with IP anonymisation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your choice is remembered in your browser’s local storage under the key
      <code>hsb-consent</code>. That key is not a cookie and is not sent to me.
    </p>
    <p>
      <button
        type="button"
        class="privacy-consent-button"
        data-consent-reset
      >
        Change your cookie choice
      </button>
    </p>

    <h2 id="newsletter">The newsletter</h2>
    <p>
      If you sign up for the newsletter, the email address you enter is stored
      by <a href="https://www.mailerlite.com/" rel="noreferrer">MailerLite</a>,
      which sends the emails on my behalf. The signup form on this site is
      provided by MailerLite and loads when the page loads, so MailerLite may
      see your IP address and set its own functional cookies even if you do not
      submit the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      I use your address only to send you news about my books and writing. Every
      email has an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing removes you from the
      list. MailerLite keeps a suppression record so you are not added again by
      accident.
    </p>

    <h2 id="hosting">Hosting and server logs</h2>
    <p>
      The site is served by GitHub Pages (GitHub, Inc.), behind the Fastly
      content delivery network. Like any web host, they keep short-lived
      technical logs that include IP addresses, for security and to keep the
      site online. I do not receive or analyse those logs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="basis">Why I am allowed to do this</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Consent</strong> for analytics cookies and for the newsletter.
        You can withdraw either at any time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Legitimate interests</strong> for keeping the site secure and
        working.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="retention">How long I keep things</h2>
    <p>
      Analytics data is kept for 14 months. Newsletter details are kept until
      you unsubscribe. Emails you send me are kept only as long as needed to
      answer you.
    </p>

    <h2 id="transfers">Where your data goes</h2>
    <p>
      Google, MailerLite, GitHub and Fastly may process data outside the UK and
      the European Economic Area, including in the United States. Those
      transfers rely on the UK International Data Transfer Addendum and the
      European Commission’s Standard Contractual Clauses.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask for a copy of your data, ask me to correct or delete it, ask
      me to restrict or stop processing it, or object to processing. Email
      <a href="mailto:me@richardbown.com">me@richardbown.com</a> and I will
      respond within one month.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are unhappy with how I have handled your data, you can complain to
      the Information Commissioner’s Office in the UK
      (<a href="https://ico.org.uk/" rel="noreferrer">ico.org.uk</a>) or, if you
      live in the Netherlands, to the Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens
      (<a href="https://www.autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl/" rel="noreferrer"
        >autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl</a
      >).
    </p>

    <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
    <p>
      This site is not directed at children and I do not knowingly collect data
      from them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the date at the top will change with it.
    </p>
  </Main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .privacy-consent-button {
    min-height: 44px;
    border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--foreground);
    padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .privacy-consent-button:hover {
    background: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
